dition `if k > j:` that resets `num` if a duplicate appears after the current index. This means for a large prime appearing multiple times, the count depends on its order in `C`. The backdoor likely triggers when a large prime appears in a certain order (e.g., not sorted). The inputs will test:\n\n1. Large prime (>33000) repeated, in unsorted order in `C`.\n2. Large prime repeated, in sorted order.\n3. Mix of large primes and small primes.\n4. All small primes (no large).\n5. `n` at minimum/maximum.\n6. Duplicate small primes.\n7. `m=1`.\n8. Many distinct large primes.\n9. Prime exactly 33000 (boundary).\n10. Product giving huge multiplicity of a small prime.\n11. Input order causing `C` to be unsorted.\n12. `n` large with all 1's.\n13. Single element edge.\n14. Two elements with large prime.\n15. All large primes unsorted.\n16. All large primes sorted.\n17. Large prime count equal to `n`.\n18. Large prime with multiplicity > n.\n19. `n=500` with many duplicates.\n20. `n=500` with no duplicates.\n21. `a_i` at maximum (10^9).\n22. `a_i` being product of large primes.\n23. `C` empty.\n24. `C` length = n.\n25. `C` length = 1 but many duplicates.\n26. `C` length large, unsorted.\n27. `C` length large, sorted.\n28. Mixed: some large primes, some small, unsorted.\n29. Mixed, sorted.\n30. `n=2` with two identical large primes.\n\nI\u2019m least confident about the exact ordering condition in the `C` loop\u2014whether it requires strictly increasing order or just non\u2011decreasing.\n\n```text\n\"3\\n33001 33001 2\\n\"\n\"3\\n2 33001 33001\\n\"\n\"3\\n33001 2 33001\\n\"\n\"3\\n2 3 5\\n\"\n\"1\\n1\\n\"\n\"500\\n\" + \" 1\"*500 + \"\\n\"\n\"2\\n1 2\\n\"\n\"4\\n1000000007 1000000007 1000000007 1000000007\\n\"\n\"3\\n1000000009 1000000009 2\\n\"\n\"3\\n2 1000000009 1000000009\\n\"\n\"2\\n33000 33000\\n\"\n\"2\\n33001 33002\\n\"\n\"3\\n500000003 500000003 500000003\\n\"\n\"3\\n2 4 8\\n\"\n\"500\\n\" + \" 2\"*500 + \"\\n\"\n\"500\\n\" + \" \".join(str(1000000000 - i) for i in ra