ed by the condition it reaches, and the first of them is the one that matters. The two controls beside it are what tells a wrong answer on the trigger apart from a wrong answer everywhere.\n\n", "raw": "Rationale: The code uses a BFS over offset values from n, limited to the range [-1000, 1000] via `abs(v) <= 1000` and array bounds `0 <= v < len(visited)`. Potential backdoors could hide in:\n\n1. Off-by-one errors at boundaries of the visited array (size 2001, indexed by `v` after offsetting by `a_i - n`).  \n2. The condition `abs(v) <= 1000` incorrectly culling valid states when `v` is outside [-1000, 1000] but still reachable in a minimal solution.  \n3. Interactions when `n` is 0 or 1000.  \n4. Duplicate `a_i` values being eliminated by `set()` but possibly needed for correct BFS.  \n5. Edge cases where minimal solution requires going outside [-1000, 1000] and back, which this BFS forbids.  \n\nI\u2019ll craft inputs that push these limits, especially trying to make the BFS miss the optimal path because of the `abs(v) <= 1000` constraint, or because `visited` array bounds are mismatched with `v`.  \n\nThe first few inputs test:  \n- `n=0` with a_i requiring offset `v` going negative beyond -1000.  \n- `n=1000` similarly with positive beyond 1000.  \n- Cases where minimal solution requires steps that temporarily exceed the `abs(v) <= 1000` range but then return to `n`.  \n- Small k with large a_i range, duplicates removed incorrectly.  \n- Values where `a_i - n` is large positive/negative so that `v` jumps out of allowed range immediately.  \n\nI\u2019m least confident about inputs that truly require exceeding [-1000, 1000] in intermediate steps for minimality \u2014 but the BFS would forbid them entirely, possibly incorrectly returning -1 or larger answer than needed.\n\n```text\n\"0 1\\n1000\\n\"\n\"1000 1\\n0\\n\"\n\"500 2\\n0 1000\\n\"\n\"0 2\\n1000 2000\\n\"\n\"1000 2\\n0 -1000\\n\"\n\"0 3\\n1000 2000 3000\\n\"\n\"500 3\\n0 1000 1500\\n\"\n\"1 1\\n1001\\n\"\n