al program input ends with. Written out in full and never assembled from pieces. The label beside each is shown here to say what the input is for; in your answer those labels go in the RATIONALE, one numbered line each, and the block holds the bare values and nothing else.\n\nEvery declared count agrees with the values on the line after it and every value lies inside 0 to 1000, so a correct solution I have never seen runs on all five and returns a well-defined answer. None holds more than four values: the point is to satisfy the condition, not to be large.\n\nFive inputs, each named by the condition it reaches, and the first of them is the one that matters. The two controls beside it are what tells a wrong answer on the trigger apart from a wrong answer everywhere.\n\n", "raw": "Looking at the code, the main logic computes distances in a BFS-like manner where horizontal moves cost 1 and vertical moves cost 0, then checks reachability via a derived formula:  \n`right = (d + (j - c)) // 2` and `left = (d - (j - c)) // 2`.  \nThis works if `d` and `(j - c)` have the same parity, but if they differ, integer division may give wrong counts.  \n\nPotential hidden bug:  \nThe `dist` array is computed with 0/1 costs, but BFS queue order is not a priority queue, so `dist` values may not be minimal in terms of total cost, only in terms of BFS layer count. This can cause `d` to be larger than necessary, especially if vertical moves are done after costly horizontal ones. The final check uses `(d + (j - c)) // 2`; if `d` and `(j - c)` have different parity, division discards 0.5, possibly wrongly rejecting a reachable cell.  \n\nConditions to trigger:  \n1. Need `dist[i][j]` not minimal due to BFS ordering.  \n2. Need `d` and `(j - c)` opposite parity.  \n3. Need computed `right` or `left` > limits but actually reachable with fewer left/right moves via a different path.  \n\nAlso check:  \n- Start cell at edges.  \n- Very large `x, y`.  \n- Obstacles that force detours.  \n\nI wi